# Setting up a new research project

This repo provides a complete scaffolding for empirical research projects optimized for Claude Code workflows:

1. **A `CLAUDE.md`** — permanent instructions that Claude reads at the start of every session. Defines ground rules, session protocol, and working relationship.

2. **Path-scoped rules** (`.claude/rules/`) — context-aware rules that load automatically. R conventions only load when working on R files; LaTeX rules only load when editing the paper.

3. **A script-registry skill** — tracks which R Markdown scripts produce which outputs via YAML front-matter. Auto-generates dependency graphs and detects drift between declared and actual I/O.

4. **Memory and task tracking** — `MEMORY.md` indexes cross-session context (methodology decisions, data quirks); `PINBOARD.md` tracks tasks and ideas.

5. **A project setup script** — available in both PowerShell (Windows) and Bash (Mac/Linux). Creates the standardised directory structure, copies in all template files, and gives you a clean starting point.

## What's in This Repo

```
├── README                          # This file
├── setup_project.ps1               # Setup script for Windows (PowerShell)
├── setup_project.sh                # Setup script for Mac/Linux (Bash)
├── PowerShell_Setup_Guide.md       # Detailed step-by-step Windows walkthrough
└── templates/
    ├── CLAUDE.md                   # Session instructions (ground rules + protocol)
    ├── MEMORY.md                   # Cross-session memory index
    ├── PINBOARD.md                 # Task tracking board
    ├── gitignore_template          # .gitignore for R + LaTeX projects
    ├── settings_local.json         # Baseline Claude Code permissions (R + Git)
    ├── build_registry.R            # Script registry builder (R)
    ├── REGISTRY_header.md          # Empty registry placeholder
    └── rules/
        ├── r-data-analysis.md      # R coding conventions (loads for Code/**, Data/**)
        ├── paper-editing.md        # LaTeX conventions (loads for Paper/**)
        └── output-conventions.md   # Output formats (loads for Output/**)
```


---

## Quick Start

### 1. Install Git (if you don't have it)

- **Windows:** Download from https://git-scm.com/download/win and run the installer. Accept all defaults.
- **Mac:** Open Terminal and type `git --version`. If it's not installed, macOS will prompt you to install it.

### 2. Clone this repo somewhere convenient

**PowerShell (Windows):**
```powershell
git clone https://github.com/aspi6246/Claude-Code-NewProject.git "$HOME\Templates\claude-tools"
```

**Bash (Mac/Linux):**
```bash
git clone https://github.com/aspi6246/Claude-Code-NewProject.git ~/Templates/claude-tools
```

### 3. (Windows only) Allow PowerShell to run scripts

PowerShell blocks scripts by default. Run this once:

```powershell
Set-ExecutionPolicy -ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned -Scope CurrentUser
```

Type `Y` when prompted.

### 4. Create a new project

**PowerShell (Windows):**
```powershell
& "$HOME\Templates\claude-tools\setup_project.ps1" -ProjectName "My-New-Paper" -DestPath "C:\Users\YourName\Dropbox\1. Projects with Claude access"
```

**Bash (Mac/Linux):**
```bash
bash ~/Templates/claude-tools/setup_project.sh My-New-Paper ~/Dropbox/Research
```

This creates the full project directory structure with all configuration files.

### 5. Fill in the placeholders

Open `CLAUDE.md` in the new project folder and replace every `XXXX`, `AUTHOR_NAME`, and `INSERT` with the real project details. Do the same for `README.md`. Or have Claude Code do this in a new session.

Claude reads your CLAUDE.md automatically. You're working.

---

## The Project Structure

Every project created by the setup script has this layout:

```
My-New-Paper/
├── CLAUDE.md                  # Instructions for Claude (read every session)
├── README.md                  # Project overview for humans
├── MEMORY.md                  # Cross-session memory index
├── PINBOARD.md                # Task and idea tracking board
├── .gitignore                 # Keeps build artifacts and large files out of Git
├── .claude/
│   ├── rules/                 # Path-scoped rules (auto-loaded by context)
│   │   ├── r-data-analysis.md # R conventions (loads for *.R, *.Rmd, Code/**, Data/**)
│   │   ├── paper-editing.md   # LaTeX conventions (loads for *.tex, *.bib, Paper/**)
│   │   └── output-conventions.md # Output formats (loads for Output/**)
│   └── settings.local.json    # Baseline permissions for R + Git commands
├── Code/
│   ├── REGISTRY.md            # Auto-generated script dependency map
│   ├── _Archive/              # Superseded scripts (never deleted, moved here)
│   ├── _Claude Logs/          # Session progress logs (key for continuity)
│   └── _Claude Scripts/       # Purled .R copies + build_registry.R
│       └── _Archive/
├── Data/
│   ├── Raw/                   # Original source data (permanently read-only)
│   └── Processed/             # Cleaned and constructed datasets
│       └── _Archive/
├── Output/
│   ├── Tables/                # LaTeX tables, CSVs
│   │   └── _Archive/
│   ├── Figures/               # PDFs, PNGs
│   │   └── _Archive/
│   └── Processed Data/        # Intermediate data (script-to-script)
├── Literature/                # Reference papers and data documentation
├── Paper/                     # LaTeX paper and .bib files
└── Admin/                     # Notes, ideas, miscellaneous
```

### Key Design Decisions

**`_Archive/` subfolders instead of deletion.** Nothing is ever deleted. When a script, dataset, or output is superseded, it gets moved to the `_Archive/` folder within the same directory. You can always recover old work.

**Path-scoped rules instead of monolithic config.** Instead of loading all conventions for every interaction, rules are split by context. R coding rules only load when you're working on R files; LaTeX rules only load when editing the paper. This keeps Claude's context window lean.

**Script registry for dependency tracking.** Each paper-producing `Code/*.Rmd` carries a `registry:` YAML block listing its inputs and outputs. The `build_registry.R` script auto-generates a dependency graph and flags drift between declared and actual I/O. The `script-registry` skill definition lives in the global skills repo ([Claude-Code-Skills-for-Academics](https://github.com/aspi6246/Claude-Code-Skills-for-Academics)); only the builder script is project-local.

**Memory system for cross-session context.** `MEMORY.md` indexes persistent memories (methodology decisions, data quirks, co-author preferences) that carry between Claude Code sessions. Session logs document _what happened_; memory captures _what matters going forward_.

**`_Claude Logs/` for session continuity.** At the end of each Claude Code session, Claude writes a progress log documenting what was done, what decisions were made, and what's still pending. The next session reads these logs first, so context carries over.

**`Data/Raw/` is read-only by convention.** Original data is never modified. All transformations happen in code and write to `Data/Processed/`. This keeps the pipeline reproducible.

**Numbered scripts for pipeline order.** Scripts in `Code/` are prefixed `01_`, `02_`, etc. to indicate execution order. This makes the dependency chain explicit.

**`PINBOARD.md` for task tracking.** Quick notes, ideas, and to-do items that persist across sessions. Claude can read and update this via the `/pinboard` command.

## How the CLAUDE.md Works

Claude Code automatically reads any `CLAUDE.md` file in the project root at the start of a session. The template includes:

- **Session protocol** — spin-up/wrap-up workflow, PINBOARD triage
- **Ground rules** — don't delete files, don't touch raw data, always confirm before acting
- **Working relationship** — defines Claude as a thinking partner who should push back and critique
- **Memory prompt** — save methodology decisions for future sessions
- **Script registry reference** — link to the registry system

The path-scoped rules in `.claude/rules/` add context-specific detail:

- **r-data-analysis.md** — R coding conventions, library stack, data handling, registry YAML
- **paper-editing.md** — LaTeX conventions, Git/Overleaf workflow
- **output-conventions.md** — table/figure formats, destination rules

You customise these for each project by filling in the placeholder text (`XXXX`, `AUTHOR_NAME`).

## Requirements

- Git
- Claude Code
- **Windows:** PowerShell
- **Mac/Linux:** Bash

## Detailed Windows Walkthrough

If you're new to Git and the command line, see `PowerShell_Setup_Guide.md` for a step-by-step guide covering everything from installing Git to creating your first project.
